@node Purpose
@section Purpose

@cindex intrinsic
@cindex extrinsic
@sysname{} provides a complete implementation of the @commonlisp{}
package system as well some parts of the Symbols dictionary.  It can be
used as either an @dfn{extrinsic} system in a @commonlisp{}
implementation that already has its own package system, or as an
@dfn{intrinsic} system to provide a package system in a new
@commonlisp{} implementation. @xref{Implementations}.

@node Architecture
@section Architecture

As mentioned in TODO, users of @sysname{} may several different purposes
each of which requires a different interaction between the user and
@sysname{}.  To facilitate those different ways of using the system,
@sysname{} provides multiple protocol which are tailored to specific
purposes:

@itemize

@item
The basic protocol consists of the @commonlisp{} package system variables,
condition types and operators.  This protocol may be used in an intrisic
or extrinsic (TODO reference or explain here?) context.  Users will use
apply the ordinary @commonlisp{} package system operators and not care much
about @sysname{}'s internals or even its existence.

@item
Some user may which too hook into package system functionality, for
example to monitor or modify the respective behavior.  Since the @commonlisp{}
package system operators are not customizable, @sysname{} provides a
client parameter customizable generic functions which implement the
behavior of package system operations.  Each @commonlisp{} package system
operator provided by @sysname{} calls the corresponding generic function
so that users can extend or change the behavior without any change to
the interface provided to clients which just use the @commonlisp{} package
system operators.

@item
The goal of the third group of users is implement symbols and packages
with custom data structures without changing the behavior of the package
system.  One such user could be a @commonlisp{} implementation that uses
@sysname{} intrinsically as its implementation of the package system.
Another user could need a simulated package system within an existing
@commonlisp{} implementation, for example for the purpose of statically
analyzing @commonlisp{} code or for sandboxing.

@end itemize

